 "nodes": [
  {
   "id": 1,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 20,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "SEIZURE AND CONTROL. The balanced random net, asynchronous at a few hertz, with its inhibitory cells silenced for two seconds from three seconds in: recruitment runs away into synchronous volleys, and when inhibition returns the population settles back. The same wiring as the balanced net; nothing is added for the seizure.",
    "width": 340,
    "near": ""
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": 2,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 230,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "This is a mechanism, not a clinical simulation: a runaway of recurrent excitation once inhibition fails, which is one of the things an epileptic focus can be. It says nothing about any patient. The block is a hyperpolarising current on the inhibitory cells, the model's version of a GABA antagonist on a slice.",
    "width": 340,
    "near": "the block"
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": 3,
   "type": "note",
   "x": -380,
   "y": 440,
   "on": true,
   "name": "",
   "params": {
    "text": "TRY THIS: change the block's width (two seconds) or its current (-150), or make the block partial with -30 and watch the rate rise without the volleys. Measured at density: 5 Hz before, volleys of 33 Hz during, and 5 Hz again within a second of the block ending. The chart draws the excitatory rate over the last ten seconds.",
    "width": 340,
    "near": "the block"
   },
   "inputs": []
  },
